INT. RADIOLOGY READING ROOM - NIGHT

Dark. Banks of monitors glow blue-white. A single chair, a headset, a cold coffee gone to skin.

DR. ADEBAYO KOWALSKI-NNAMDI (40s) sits hunched at the center workstation, scrolling through a stack of chest films. He rubs his eyes. He scrolls. He stops.

He leans in. Zooms. Zooms again.

He taps a key. The dictation system plays back his earlier note through the speakers.

ADEBAYO (V.O.)
(recorded)
Chest, two views. Lungs clear. No acute findings.

He frowns. Pans the image. A small shadow, high in the left lung, edges crisp. He measures it. Measures it again. He leans back and exhales slowly.

ADEBAYO
(to himself)
Not clear.

He deletes the recording. The speakers click. He picks up the mic and begins to speak, steady and low.

ADEBAYO (CONT'D)
(dictating)
Chest, two views. Small nodule, left upper lobe, six millimeters. Recommend follow-up CT.

He sets the mic down and rolls his shoulders. On the screen, the shadow waits.
